See what’s going on with tempdb

Run this on the server:

USE tempdb;
GO

SELECT 
    name,
    size/128.0       AS CurrentSizeMB,
    max_size, 
    max_size/128.0   AS MaxSizeMB,
    growth,
    is_percent_growth,
    physical_name
FROM sys.database_files;


Interpret:

CurrentSizeMB = current file size

MaxSizeMB = max size allowed

-1 or very large → essentially unlimited (up to 16 TB or disk size)

If CurrentSizeMB == MaxSizeMB → you hit the cap

is_percent_growth = 0 → growth in MB (good), 1 → percent (bad)

Also check Windows free space on the volume where tempdb lives.

2. Common causes & fixes
Scenario A – Max size reached

If CurrentSizeMB is the same as MaxSizeMB and there’s still free disk:

Increase max size or set to unlimited (careful with disk):

ALTER DATABASE tempdb 
MODIFY FILE (
    NAME = tempdev,       -- repeat for all data files
    MAXSIZE = UNLIMITED   -- or a higher explicit size, e.g. 51200MB
);
GO


Also make sure autogrowth is reasonable (you mentioned 500 MB – that’s fine):

ALTER DATABASE tempdb 
MODIFY FILE (
    NAME = tempdev,
    FILEGROWTH = 500MB
);
GO


Do this for every tempdb data file, same size & same growth.

Scenario B – Autogrowth disabled

If growth = 0, the file can’t grow:

ALTER DATABASE tempdb 
MODIFY FILE (
    NAME = tempdev,
    FILEGROWTH = 500MB  -- or 512MB / 1024MB, your choice
);
GO


Again, repeat for each data file and ensure the log has growth too.

Scenario C – Disk is actually full

If MaxSizeMB still has room, but Windows shows little or no free space on the drive:

Add more disk space to that volume

Or move tempdb to a larger / faster drive

Or shrink/move other databases off that drive

Move tempdb example (requires restart):

USE master;
GO

ALTER DATABASE tempdb 
MODIFY FILE (
    NAME = tempdev,
    FILENAME = 'E:\SQLData\tempdb.mdf'
);

ALTER DATABASE tempdb 
MODIFY FILE (
    NAME = templog,
    FILENAME = 'E:\SQLLog\templog.ldf'
);
GO
-- Then restart SQL Server service

3. Longer-term tempdb tuning (so jobs stop failing)

Since this happened during a job doing a dbo.SORT, I’d also:

Pre-size tempdb so it rarely grows:

Set initial size based on observed peak usage (for example, 20–30 GB total if your workload needs it).

Use multiple data files with same size/growth (e.g., 4 or 8 files depending on CPU and contention).

Keep autogrowth ON with a fixed MB increment (500–1024 MB).

Put tempdb on fast SSD/NVMe with enough free space.

Example starting point for 4 data files:

ALTER DATABASE tempdb 
MODIFY FILE (NAME = tempdev, SIZE = 8192MB, FILEGROWTH = 500MB);
ALTER DATABASE tempdb 
MODIFY FILE (NAME = temp2,   SIZE = 8192MB, FILEGROWTH = 500MB);
ALTER DATABASE tempdb 
MODIFY FILE (NAME = temp3,   SIZE = 8192MB, FILEGROWTH = 500MB);
ALTER DATABASE tempdb 
MODIFY FILE (NAME = temp4,   SIZE = 8192MB, FILEGROWTH = 500MB);

ALTER DATABASE tempdb 
MODIFY FILE (NAME = templog, SIZE = 4096MB, FILEGROWTH = 256MB);


Adjust sizes to your environment.
